 "criteria": [
  {
   "detail": "10 tableaux: worst triple sum 2.22e-16, worst shift on changing the base country 2.22e-16, worst residual-design inner product 2.11e-15. Transitivity and base invariance are what that paper claims and they hold to machine precision",
   "kind": "known_answer",
   "name": "B81-1",
   "passed": true,
   "statement": "the two properties that paper claims for the estimator: the parities are transitive and they do not move with the base region"
  },
  {
   "detail": "10 tableaux: every cycle of the bipartite graph sums to 2.22e-16 on the fitted values and to 2.207 or more on the raw log prices. The cycle count equals `E - V + 1` by closed form and by incidence rank in every case. **So transitivity is not a property of dummy regressions, it is that the fitted value is a difference of a potential**",
   "kind": "own_reading",
   "name": "B81-2",
   "passed": true,
   "statement": "the fitted value is a coboundary on the bipartite graph, so every cycle of that graph sums to zero on it while the raw log prices do not"
  },
  {
   "detail": "10 tableaux. The bipartite graph CPD projects on has `b1 = MR - (M+R) + 1 = (M-1)(R-1)`, closed form against incidence rank in 10 of 10, running 8 to 36. The complete country graph GEKS projects on has `(R-1)(R-2)/2`, running 1 to 15. **Two methods, one operation, two graphs, and the count of what each discards is not the same number**",
   "kind": "own_reading",
   "name": "B81-3",
   "passed": true,
   "statement": "the two methods project on two graphs, and the dimension of what each discards is (M-1)(R-1) against (R-1)(R-2)/2"
  },
  {
   "detail": "10 complete equal-weight tableaux: the worst gap between CPD's `beta_b - beta_a` and GEKS on the Jevons index is 2.00e-15, so **the two graphs give one answer there**. The routes share no code: one is an OLS on dummies, the other averages over products and then multiplies out the GEKS formula. **Where they part is the incomplete tableau**: GEKS needs every bilateral index and a bilateral index needs the two countries to price something in common, while CPD needs only that the bipartite graph is connected, which is a weaker condition and is what B81-5 counts",
   "kind": "own_reading",
   "name": "B81-4",
   "passed": true,
   "statement": "on a complete equal-weight tableau the two projections return the same parities, computed by two routes sharing no code"
  },
  {
   "detail": "40 sparse tableaux, 13 of them disconnected. The design matrix's rank deficiency equals the number of components in 40 of 40, and `b1 = E - V + c` matches the incidence rank throughout. **So whether the parities are identified at all is a connectivity question about the tableau**: one component is one free constant, and two components are two, which is a parity that cannot be read rather than one read badly",
   "kind": "own_reading",
   "name": "B81-5",
   "passed": true,
   "statement": "an incomplete tableau can disconnect, and then the parities are not identified: the design matrix's rank deficiency counts the components"
  },
  {
   "detail": "10 tableaux, dropping each dummy in turn: the fitted values move by at most 2.44e-15 while the coefficients themselves move by up to 1.139. **Both halves are required**: unchanged fitted values with unchanged coefficients would mean the gauge was not exercised",
   "kind": "instrument",
   "name": "B81-6",
   "passed": true,
   "statement": "the gauge: dropping a different dummy moves the coefficients and leaves the fitted values where they were"
  },
  {
   "detail": "B79 registered: the same reading on the country-product-dummy estimator, which is a regression rather than a projection and therefore a different loss. Answered here, and the registration's wording was half wrong: a regression onto dummies IS a projection, and the loss is the same sum of squares. What differs is the graph. B81-2 shows the fitted value is a coboundary on the bipartite product-by-country graph, B81-3 counts what each graph discards, and B81-4 shows the two answers coincide on a complete equal-weight tableau",
   "kind": "bookkeeping",
   "name": "B81-7",
   "passed": true,
   "statement": "B79's registration, named, with the correction to its wording"
  }
 ],
 "mapping": {
  "B81-1": "transitivity, base invariance and orthogonality all inside tolerance -> PASS; any outside -> FAIL",
  "B81-2": "cycles sum to zero on the fitted values AND do not on the raw prices AND the cycle count matches both the closed form and the rank -> PASS. The second clause is required: if the raw prices were transitive too, the criterion would be reading an identity",
  "B81-3": "both closed forms match their incidence ranks -> PASS; else FAIL",
  "B81-4": "the two routes agree within tolerance -> PASS; a disagreement would mean one of the two implementations is wrong, since on a complete equal-weight tableau the two projections are the same object",
  "B81-5": "rank deficiency equals the component count everywhere AND at least one drawn tableau is disconnected AND at least one is connected -> PASS; otherwise the sampling did not exercise the reading",
  "B81-6": "fitted values unchanged AND coefficients moved -> PASS; both unchanged means the gauge was never exercised, so that is a FAIL and not a pass",
  "B81-7": "the registration named with what answers it -> PASS"
 },
